  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThe 09Z/3 am surface map shows a cold front from central WI \nthrough far eastern Iowa into nw MO, with large band of light to\nmoderate rain along and behind the cold front and nearing the\nIA/IL border. Satellite imagery shows mid level clouds\nincreasing over central and southeast IL early this morning.\nMilder temps were mostly in the upper 40s to mid 50s, but range\nfrom 41F at Robinson to 57F at Galesburg and Jacksonville with\nlower 60s from Quincy and Pittsfield sw. \n\nThe cold front to be near the IL river by mid morning, getting \nnear I-57 early this afternoon and through Lawrence county in \nfar southeast IL by sunset. The rain band lasting about 4 hours to\npush as far east as I-55 by 8-9 am, near I-70 by noon and into \nLawrence county by 3 pm. Precipitable Water (PWATs) values \npeak between 1-1.3 inches for a few hours with the rain band as\nit passes through during today, and models continue to increase\nQPF amounts a bit more with NBM generally showing a quarter to \nhalf inch over CWA today. Locally up to three quarter inches \npossible in west central IL. SPC as general risk of thunderstorms\nsouth of I-70 this afternoon, though instability looks quite \nweak over CWA today even in southeast IL, so kept thunder \nchances out. Highs today range from mid to upper 50s over the IL\nriver valley to mid to upper 60s in southeast IL. But breezy nw\nwinds 15-25 mph behind the cold front to cool temps into the \nmid to upper 40s from I-72 north by sunset and mid to upper 50s \nsoutheast of I-70. \n\nRain showers in southeast IL late this afternoon to \ndiminish/end during early evening. Otherwise dry and colder \ntonight with clouds diminishing over the IL river valley late \nthis afternoon and over the rest of CWA during this evening.\nSubfreezing lows in upper 20s to around 30F late tonight and\nbrisk NW winds to make it feel colder. Strengthening Canadian\nhigh pressure 1030-1035 mb building into the Midwest on Monday\nto bring a fair amount of sunshine but NW winds to keep it\nseasonably cold. Highs Monday in the low to mid 40s central IL\nand upper 40s in southeast IL southeast of I-70. Skies become\npartly cloudy overnight Monday night and cold lows in the lower\n20s (mid 20s from highway 50 south). Partly to mostly cloudy\nskies Tue with highs around 40F from I-55 nw and mid 40s se of\nI-70. Lows Tue night in the low to mid 20s. \n\nA southern stream system moving over the southern MS river\nvalley by dawn Wed while a northern stream cutoff low drops down\nfrom western Canada into the MO valley on Wed. The medium range\nmodels continue to trend further west with QPF moving NNW into\nIL Wed afternoon and Wed night and then diminishing Thu night. \nThe cutoff low moving into MO late Wed is helping lift moisture \nNNW into IL and Ecmwf, GFS and GEM are all showing this now.\nPlus it appears cold enough for rain/snow mixture especially Wed\nnight into Thu morning with WPC has minor snow accumulations\nover central and even parts of southeast IL as far south the\ncounties along I-70. The strongest forcing and highest moisture\nstill appears to affect the mid Atlantic States into New \nEngland Wed night into Friday so Thanksgiving holiday travelers \nwill need to monitor as we approaching the busiest travel week \nof the year. Temps stay seasonably cold through Friday then\nslight modification on Sat/Sunday with highs in the upper\n40s/lower 50s.",
